  <section class="wrap fiche-body">
    <div>
      <h2>The vibe</h2>
      <p>The Giens peninsula, in Hyères, offers a coastal trail that runs along wild coves, with sea views at every turn of the path. The full loop ("sentier du littoral + route des crêtes") is about 10.5 km for 3h to 3h30 of walking and +322 m total elevation gain; shorter variants exist, such as Darboussières ↔ Madrague (~7 km), handy if you're after a quicker outing with your dog.</p>

      <h2>What to know before you go</h2>
      <p>The level is easy to moderate: good shoes are recommended for the rocky, sometimes slippery sections, and it's best avoided in strong wind or rain (some sections overlook the cliff). On dogs: many accounts describe this trail as noticeably more accessible to dogs than the nearby island of Port-Cros (banned to dogs even on a leash in the national park's core zone) — but no official regulation could be found explicitly stating "leash mandatory"; to be safe, keep your dog on a leash, especially near busy coves and sections overlooking the cliff. Shade varies a lot by section: more generous on the pine-wood side of La Madrague and Les Darboussières, almost absent on the rockier, more exposed western corniche. No water point is reported along the route: bring at least 2 litres of water per person, and enough to let your dog drink regularly, as the sand can become scorching for its paws in summer.</p>
      <div class="callout">Coco's tip: set off early in the morning in summer to avoid the heat on the shadeless sections, and check access (the area can be restricted by prefectural order during droughts, like the Var's other massifs).</div>

      <h2>Nearby</h2>
      <p>Hyères and its beaches are nearby, a good option to end the day with a swim, notably the <a href="/en/fiche-hyeres-merou">Mérou dog beach</a>. On site, a <a href="/en/fiche-toiletteur-pat-toutou">groomer</a> and the <a href="/en/fiche-camping-mediterranee-hyeres">Camping Méditerranée</a> round out a stay in the area nicely.</p>
    </div>

